lima/ppir: fuse fadd(fsum2(a), b) into the vec4 accumulator's op 0x12 (sum2add)

This is tools/malitrace/lima-patches/dot2-add.patch, rebased onto 26.2.6 + 0001-0010 and with the
op's semantics now confirmed from the blob two ways. #375 dropped it for moving reference UI polygon-fill
edge pixels; the numbers below say those pixels were wrong before, not after.

Blob evidence, the encoding. capture/games/compare-26.2.6/prog-6.blob.pp.bin, every edge of the
polygon fill `dot(u_e.xy, v_px) + u_e.z` is one instruction; decoded field by field with
beat-shader/ppdec.py (lima's disassembler drops arg1 of an op it does not know and printed only
`op18.v1 $1.x ^v0.xyyy`):
    varying  alignment=1 index=0 dest=$0 mask=3            (v_px.xy)
    uniform  alignment=2 index=4                           (u_e3)
    vec4_mul op=0 arg0=uniform.xyyy arg1=$0.xyyy mask=0    (-> ^v0: ex*x, ey*y)
    vec4_acc op=0x12 mul_in=1 arg0=^v0.xyyy arg1=uniform.zzzz dest=$1 mask=1
So op 0x12 is dest.x = arg0.x + arg0.y + arg1.x, and arg1 is a real source (its swizzle zzzz selects
u_e.z). The alternative readings are excluded by the picture: with arg1 ignored the term +u_e.z
disappears and |m| is in the hundreds everywhere, so clamp(m+0.5) would be 0 or 1 over the whole
quad; with arg0.xyz summed the swizzle xyyy would double ey*y.

Blob evidence, the compiler. libGLES_mali.so r6p0-01rel1 ("Mali online shader compiler r6p0-01rel1
[Revision 96995]") maps its backend IR opcode to the hardware's accumulator opcode in the function at
0x000e56e0 (a 58-entry jump table at 0xe56fc, indexed by [node+4] - 2). IR 9, 10 and 11 land on
hardware 0x10, 0x11 and 0x12 at 0xe590c, 0xe58fc and 0xe58ec; 0x10 and 0x11 are lima's known sum3 and
sum4, so 0x12 is the third member of that family. All three are emitted only when the second argument
of the mapper is zero (`cmp r1,#0; mvnne lr,#0; moveq lr,#18`), the same guard the horizontal sums
carry, which is why this patch keeps the unshifted-destination encoding sum3/sum4 use.

What lima did. lima_nir_algebraic lowered every fsum2 to a scalar fadd of its two components, so each
edge cost a uniform move plus two scalar multiply-add pairs.

Effect. reference UI polygon fill (capture/games program 6, shared by Games, Photos, Home and Clock): 11 -> 7
PP instructions, the blob's count, 69 -> 41 code words, and the edge instruction matches the blob's
field for field except the unused .zw lanes of mul.v0 and the destination register. Demo and Photos
unchanged (they do not use that shader's shape).
Scenes: Home draws it 215 times a frame (home-swipe) and 145 (home-idle), Clock 21, Games 8. The
saving is exactly 4 instructions x the pixels it covers. From the blob's own counter the covered area
is at most counter/7, so the bound per frame is Home <= 3.2 ms, Clock <= 2.3 ms, and for Games the
covered area is measured (968 px) so it is 0.003 ms. Home and Clock have no memory dump in the
capture, so their true coverage is not measured here.

Pictures: NOT BIT-IDENTICAL, and that is the point. PP registers are fp16. lima computes
(ex*x + ez) + ey*y, rounding a large intermediate (|ez| is half the element's size in pixels, so tens
to a thousand, where an fp16 step is 0.03 to 1.0); the blob's op 0x12 rounds once. A float16 model of
both forms against float64 (beat-shader/out/sum2add-rounding.txt) gives this worst-case alpha error on
edge pixels, by element half-extent: 61 px 17/255, 122 px 32/255, 300 px 71/255, 540 px 132/255,
960 px 156/255 for lima, and 0/255 for op 0x12. #375 saw 16 edge pixels move by up to 82/255 against
lima build A, which is in that range for an element a few hundred pixels wide: the patch moves those
pixels onto the blob's values. The model assumes the accumulator keeps the two products at the
multiplier's precision and rounds once; if it rounds each product first, op 0x12 is still one rounding
of a large value better than lima's. HARDWARE CHECK MUST BE AGAINST AN ANDROID SHOT, not against
lima's own build (hardware change 407).

Applies to Mesa 26.2.6 + 0001-0010.
